  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Tuesday)\nIssued at 132 PM CDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nShower and storm chances will continue in the vicinity of the\nfrontal zone Wednesday and Thursday, which is likely to lie from \nSE-NW over the eastern portion of the forecast area. PoPs will still\nbe low on Wednesday and confined mainly to the east. Moisture \npooling near the boundary is also likely to result in higher \ndewpoints. With forecast highs still expected to be near triple\ndigits over most of the area, forecast heat indices are expected\nto reach advisory level across E OK and the lower Ark River\nValley. A few locations may touch 110F, but elected to hold off on\na warning given uncertainties with cloud cover. Heat headlines may\nbe needed for portions of the area Thursday and Friday as well\ndepending on how things shake out with clouds and storms.\n\nIndications in the CAMs and GFS suggest an uptick in coverage \nWednesday night into Thursday morning before the boundary either \nwashes out or retreats north out of the area and with an upper \nwave Thursday night into Friday morning.\n\nA weather pattern change is expected by the end of the week, and\nwill bring big positive changes. The H5 ridge center retrogrades\nback to the Desert Southwest while a shortwave trough drops\nsoutheast over the heart of the CONUS. This system will force a\nmore noticeable cool front south across the region Friday, with \nlow storm chances associated with the boundary. Below average \ncooler and more importantly less humid weather is expected behind \nthe front for the upcoming weekend.\n\nLacy",
